{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["He said <b>When you sacrifice a sacrifice of peace-offerings</b> — for after He forbade slaughter and all divine service to idols and molten gods, and assigned all the services entirely to the unique Name, He said that when you sacrifice to HaShem, you shall not sacrifice to Him except לִרְצוֹנְכֶם (for your acceptance), that your service should be acceptable before Him, and He will accept you, just as a servant finds favor with his master when he does all that he is commanded, in the language of \"and it shall be accepted for him to make atonement for him\" (Leviticus 1:4), \"for the light of Your face, for You were pleased with them\" (Psalms 44:4). And the reason is that you should not think there is any benefit in foreign worship, nor should you perform the honored service of HaShem on condition to receive reward, but rather to do His will — for His simple will is what is fitting and obligatory. And He says this concerning peace-offerings, since they are lesser offerings eaten by the owners themselves, that they should be careful in their intention to be with acceptable intent — how much more so in the holy of holies offerings. Or because peace-offerings are the attribute of Israel, as our Rabbis said (Zevaḥim 116b), the children of Noah did not offer peace-offerings, וְהַמַּשְׂכִּיל יָבִין. And our Rabbis said (Ḥullin 13a): לִרְצוֹנְכֶם זְבוֹחוּ — slaughter of your own volition, for one who is distracted in sacred offerings renders them invalid."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The Talmud citations use the chapter/page format as they appear in the source; Zevaḥim 116 is standardly cited as 116b, Ḥullin 13 as 13a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:54.388036189Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}